-- title:
--   Crossing point of the special fibre of X₀(Mp) is supersingular
-- statement:
--   Fix a prime $p$ and an integer $M \ge 5$ with $p \nmid M$. Let $L$ be a field of characteristic zero that is a $p$-th cyclotomic extension of $\mathbb{Q}$, and $\zeta \in L$ a primitive $p$-th root of unity. Let $K_2$ be the intermediate field of $L \subseteq \mathrm{LaurentSeries}\,L$ given by [`ModularCurve.laurentBaseChange`](def/ModularCurve_LaurentCoeff.html#L103), that is, the subfield generated over $L$ by the coefficientwise image under $\mathbb{Q} \to L$ of [`ModularCurve.qExpFunctionFieldC ℚ (Gamma0 (M*p))`](def/ModularCurve_X1.html#L101), the subfield of $\mathrm{LaurentSeries}\,\mathbb{Q}$ generated over $\mathbb{Q}$ by the quotients of integral $q$-expansions of modular forms for $\Gamma_0(Mp)$. Let $A$ be a discrete valuation domain with fraction field $L$, such that $p$ lies in the maximal ideal of $A$ and $\zeta$ lies in the image of $A \to L$, with $K_2$ an $A$-algebra compatibly with $A \to L \to K_2$, and let $\varpi$ generate the maximal ideal of $A$. Let $j \in K_2$ be nonzero and have image in $\mathrm{LaurentSeries}\,L$ the coefficientwise image of the $q$-expansion [`ModularCurve.jq`](def/ModularCurve_X0.html#L157), and write $B =$ `chartAlgFin A K₂ j` for the $A$-subalgebra of $K_2$ of elements integral over $A[j]$. Let $y \subseteq B$ be a prime ideal containing the image of $\varpi$ and containing two distinct minimal primes of the ideal of $B$ generated by that image. Then for every algebraically closed field $\Omega$ of characteristic $p$ and every ring homomorphism $\varphi : B \to \Omega$ with kernel $y$, the element $\varphi(j)$ lies in [`ModularCurve.ssJSet p Ω`](def/ModularCurve_SupersingularModuli.html#L7): every elliptic Weierstrass curve over $\Omega$ with $j$-invariant $\varphi(j)$ has no nonzero point killed by $p$.
--
--   This is the "crossing implies supersingular" direction for the integral model of $X_0(Mp)$, in ring-theoretic form: a point of the finite chart lying on two distinct components of the special fibre has supersingular $j$-invariant. It is the converse of the statement that minimal primes of the special fibre lie under supersingular points, and it is used to derive the corresponding assertion at level $X_1(Mp)$.
